{#
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  ARTICLE FURNITURE — the parts every published document shares
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  Four pages present a citable document: /philosophy, /integrity, /terms and
  /privacy. The masthead, the Copy/Download/Cite toolbar, the contents and the
  citation panel are identical on all four, and the toolbar and citation panel in
  particular are substantial markup with real accessibility obligations — a
  tablist with a roving tabindex, a popover that closes on Escape, a live region.

  Copied four times, three of those copies eventually lose the Escape handler.
  So they live here as macros and the pages pass their own metadata in.

  ── CALLING CONVENTION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  Macros cannot see the template context, so `csp_nonce` and everything else is an
  explicit argument. Every macro takes a single `d` hash rather than a positional
  list, because these have up to a dozen fields and `tools(a, b, c, '', '', d)` is
  how the wrong value ends up in the wrong slot.

  Presentation only. Not one of these macros decides anything: no figure is
  computed here, no date is read from a clock. The route resolves all of that and
  hands it over, so the page, the .txt and the .md cannot disagree.
#}

{# The shared stylesheet. A <link> rather than an inline block: four pages share it
   and it is deliberately outside AssetBundle::STYLESHEETS — see the sheet's own
   header for why. `'self'` in style-src-elem covers it, so no nonce is needed. #}
{% macro styles() %}
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ asset('/assets/css/components/article.css') }}">
{% endmacro %}

{#
  Citation metadata for machines: Highwire Press `citation_*` and Dublin Core.
  These are what Zotero, Mendeley and Google Scholar actually read when somebody
  saves a reference; without them a manager guesses the title from <title> and the
  date from nothing.

  d: {title, author, publisher, published, updated, url}
#}
{% macro cite_meta(d) %}
<meta name="citation_title" content="{{ d.title }}">
<meta name="citation_author" content="{{ d.author }}">
<meta name="citation_publisher" content="{{ d.publisher }}">
<meta name="citation_publication_date" content="{{ d.published }}">
<meta name="citation_online_date" content="{{ d.updated }}">
<meta name="citation_public_url" content="{{ d.url }}">
<meta name="DC.title" content="{{ d.title }}">
<meta name="DC.creator" content="{{ d.author }}">
<meta name="DC.publisher" content="{{ d.publisher }}">
<meta name="DC.date" content="{{ d.updated }}">
<meta name="DC.identifier" content="{{ d.url }}">
<meta name="DC.type" content="Text">
<meta name="DC.format" content="text/html">
{% endmacro %}

{#
  schema.org Article. `version` is the field that makes a dated document citable
  rather than merely dated.

  d: {title, subtitle, description, author, publisher, published, updated, version, url, terms_url}
#}
{% macro article_ld(d, nonce) %}
<script nonce="{{ nonce }}" type="application/ld+json">
{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Article",
"headline":{{ d.title|json_encode|raw }},
{% if d.subtitle is defined and d.subtitle %}"alternativeHeadline":{{ d.subtitle|json_encode|raw }},{% endif %}
"description":{{ d.description|striptags|json_encode|raw }},
"author":{"@type":"Organization","name":{{ d.author|json_encode|raw }}},
"publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":{{ d.publisher|json_encode|raw }}},
"datePublished":{{ d.published|json_encode|raw }},
"dateModified":{{ d.updated|json_encode|raw }},
"version":{{ d.version|json_encode|raw }},
"inLanguage":"en",
"isAccessibleForFree":true,
{% if d.terms_url is defined and d.terms_url %}"license":{{ d.terms_url|json_encode|raw }},{% endif %}
"mainEntityOfPage":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":{{ d.url|json_encode|raw }}},
"url":{{ d.url|json_encode|raw }}}
</script>
{% endmacro %}

{#
  The masthead: eyebrow, title, subtitle, standfirst, colophon.

  d: {eyebrow, title, subtitle, standfirst, author, version, updated, read_minutes}
#}
{% macro masthead(d) %}
<p class="ar-eyebrow">{{ d.eyebrow }}</p>
<h1 class="ar-h1">{{ d.title }}</h1>
{% if d.subtitle is defined and d.subtitle %}<p class="ar-sub">{{ d.subtitle }}</p>{% endif %}
{% if d.standfirst is defined and d.standfirst %}<p class="ar-stand">{{ d.standfirst|raw }}</p>{% endif %}
<dl class="ar-meta">
  <div><dfn>By</dfn> <b>{{ d.author }}</b></div>
  {% if d.version is defined and d.version %}<div><dfn>Version</dfn> <b>{{ d.version }}</b></div>{% endif %}
  <div><dfn>Updated</dfn> <b><time datetime="{{ d.updated }}">{{ d.updated|date('j F Y') }}</time></b></div>
  {% if d.read_minutes is defined and d.read_minutes %}<div><b>{{ d.read_minutes }} min</b> read</div>{% endif %}
</dl>
{% endmacro %}

{#
  Copy · Download · Cite.

  `@click.away` closes the download menu and Escape closes it from the keyboard —
  a popover dismissible only by pointer is a trap for anyone who opened it with a
  keyboard. The download links are real <a download> hrefs, so Download works with
  no JavaScript at all; only Copy needs it.

  d: {txt_url, md_url, file_stem, id, portable}

  `id` disambiguates the popover on a page that ever renders two toolbars.

  `portable` (default true) is whether this document HAS a .txt/.md edition. Set it
  false and only Cite renders. That is the case on /integrity, whose prose lives in
  its template rather than in PHP: a Copy that lifted a different document from the
  one on screen, or a Download that saved one, would be worse than no button —
  the reader would file it and find out later. See MethodologyDocument.
#}
{% macro tools(d) %}
{% set pid = 'ar-dl-' ~ (d.id|default('1')) %}
{% set portable = d.portable is not defined or d.portable %}
<div class="ar-tools">
  {% if portable %}
  <button type="button" class="ar-tool" :class="done === 'all' && 'is-done'" @click="copyAll()">
    <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
      <rect x="9" y="9" width="12" height="12" rx="2"/><path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"/>
    </svg>
    <span x-text="done === 'all' ? 'Copied' : 'Copy'">Copy</span>
  </button>

  <div style="position:relative">
    <button type="button" class="ar-tool" @click="menu = !menu" :aria-expanded="menu ? 'true' : 'false'"
            aria-haspopup="true" aria-controls="{{ pid }}">
      <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M12 3v12"/><path d="m7 11 5 5 5-5"/><path d="M4 21h16"/>
      </svg>
      Download
    </button>
    <div class="ar-pop" id="{{ pid }}" x-show="menu" x-cloak @click.away="menu = false"
         @keydown.escape.window="menu = false" role="menu">
      <a href="{{ d.md_url }}" download="{{ d.file_stem }}.md" role="menuitem" @click="menu = false">
        <b>Markdown (.md)</b>
        <span>Headings and quotes preserved — for quoting or republishing.</span>
      </a>
      <a href="{{ d.txt_url }}" download="{{ d.file_stem }}.txt" role="menuitem" @click="menu = false">
        <b>Plain text (.txt)</b>
        <span>Wrapped to 78 columns — pastes cleanly into an email.</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
  {% endif %}

  <a class="ar-tool" href="#cite">
    <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
      <path d="M6 4h9l5 5v11H6z"/><path d="M14 4v6h6"/><path d="M9 14h6M9 17h4"/>
    </svg>
    Cite
  </a>
</div>
<p class="ar-say" role="status" aria-live="polite" x-text="said"></p>
{% endmacro %}

{#
  Contents.

  groups: [{label, items: [{id, title}]}]  — `label` may be empty for an
  unlabelled single group. A trailing "How to cite" entry is added here so every
  document's contents ends the same way.

  `wide` picks the sticky margin rail (.ar-rail) or the inline block (.ar-toc);
  the two are mutually exclusive at 1180px in CSS, and both are rendered because
  which one applies is a viewport question, not a server one.
#}
{% macro contents(groups, wide) %}
<nav class="{{ wide ? 'ar-rail' : 'ar-toc' }}" aria-label="Document contents">
  <h2>Contents</h2>
  <ol>
    {% for g in groups %}
      {% if g.label %}<li class="ar-part" aria-hidden="true">{{ g.label }}</li>{% endif %}
      {% for it in g.items %}
        <li><a href="#{{ it.id }}">{{ it.title|striptags }}</a></li>
      {% endfor %}
    {% endfor %}
    <li class="ar-part" aria-hidden="true">Reference</li>
    <li class="ar-ref"><a href="#cite">How to cite</a></li>
  </ol>
</nav>
{% endmacro %}

{#
  A rendered philosophy/structured section body.

  Walks the block kinds emitted by CommunityVotingPhilosophy. The kinds are few on
  purpose: a format that can express anything ends up expressing it inconsistently.
#}
{% macro blocks(list) %}
  {%- for block in list %}
    {%- if block.p is defined %}
      <p>{{ block.p|raw }}</p>
    {%- elseif block.h3 is defined %}
      <h4 class="ar-h4">{{ block.h3|raw }}</h4>
    {%- elseif block.quote is defined %}
      <blockquote class="ar-quote"><p>{{ block.quote|raw }}</p></blockquote>
    {%- elseif block.list is defined %}
      <ul class="ar-list">{% for li in block.list %}<li>{{ li|raw }}</li>{% endfor %}</ul>
    {%- elseif block.steps is defined %}
      <ol class="ar-steps">{% for li in block.steps %}<li>{{ li|raw }}</li>{% endfor %}</ol>
    {%- elseif block.note is defined %}
      <div class="ar-note">
        <h4>{{ block.note.title|raw }}</h4>
        {% for p in block.note.body %}<p>{{ p|raw }}</p>{% endfor %}
      </div>
    {%- endif %}
  {%- endfor %}
{% endmacro %}

{#
  The bridge between two documents. The philosophy argues and the methodology
  enforces; each ends by handing the reader to the other, which is what makes them
  one Integrity Centre rather than two pages that happen to match.

  d: {url, kicker, title, blurb}
#}
{% macro next(d) %}
<a class="ar-next" href="{{ d.url }}">
  <span>{{ d.kicker }}</span>
  <b>{{ d.title }}</b>
  <em>{{ d.blurb }}</em>
</a>
{% endmacro %}

{#
  "How to cite", with a real tablist.

  role/aria-selected/aria-controls and a roving tabindex, so the panel is announced
  when the tab changes rather than silently swapped. Arrow keys are wired on the
  container — see the note in the script macro for why that is not optional.

  d: {citations, version, published, updated, accessed, url, txt_url, md_url, file_stem, note}
#}
{% macro cite(d) %}
<section class="ar-cite" id="cite">
  <h2>How to cite this document</h2>
  <p>{{ d.note|raw }}</p>

  <dl class="ar-meta">
    {% if d.version %}<div><dfn>Version</dfn> <b>{{ d.version }}</b></div>{% endif %}
    <div><dfn>Published</dfn> <b><time datetime="{{ d.published }}">{{ d.published|date('j F Y') }}</time></b></div>
    <div><dfn>Updated</dfn> <b><time datetime="{{ d.updated }}">{{ d.updated|date('j F Y') }}</time></b></div>
    <div><dfn>Accessed</dfn> <b><time datetime="{{ d.accessed }}">{{ d.accessed|date('j F Y') }}</time></b></div>
  </dl>

  <div class="ar-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Citation format"
       @keydown.arrow-right.prevent="moveTab('next')"
       @keydown.arrow-left.prevent="moveTab('prev')"
       @keydown.home.prevent="moveTab('home')"
       @keydown.end.prevent="moveTab('end')">
    {% for c in d.citations %}
      <button type="button" class="ar-tab" role="tab"
              id="ar-tab-{{ c.id }}" aria-controls="ar-panel-{{ c.id }}"
              :aria-selected="tab === '{{ c.id }}' ? 'true' : 'false'"
              :tabindex="tab === '{{ c.id }}' ? 0 : -1"
              @click="tab = '{{ c.id }}'">{{ c.label }}</button>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

  <div class="ar-cite-body">
    {% for c in d.citations %}
      <div role="tabpanel" id="ar-panel-{{ c.id }}" aria-labelledby="ar-tab-{{ c.id }}"
           x-show="tab === '{{ c.id }}'" x-cloak>
        <p class="ar-cite-text" x-ref="cite{{ c.id }}">{{ c.text }}</p>
        <div class="ar-cite-act">
          <button type="button" class="ar-tool" :class="done === 'cite' && 'is-done'"
                  @click="copyCite($refs.cite{{ c.id }})">
            <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
              <rect x="9" y="9" width="12" height="12" rx="2"/><path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1"/>
            </svg>
            Copy {{ c.label }}
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="ar-tool" :class="done === 'link' && 'is-done'" @click="copyLink()">
            <svg width="14" height="14" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7 0l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7-7l-1 1"/><path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7 0l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7 7l1-1"/>
            </svg>
            Copy permalink
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>

  <div class="ar-refs">
    <span>Machine-readable</span>
    <ul>
      <li>This page carries <code>citation_*</code> and Dublin Core metadata plus schema.org
          <code>Article</code> markup, so Zotero, Mendeley and similar tools can import the
          reference directly from the URL.</li>
      {% if d.portable is not defined or d.portable %}
        <li>Plain-text and Markdown editions:
            <a href="{{ d.txt_url }}" download="{{ d.file_stem }}.txt">{{ d.file_stem }}.txt</a> ·
            <a href="{{ d.md_url }}" download="{{ d.file_stem }}.md">{{ d.file_stem }}.md</a></li>
      {% elseif d.portable_note is defined and d.portable_note %}
        <li>{{ d.portable_note|raw }}</li>
      {% endif %}
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>
{% endmacro %}

{#
  The Alpine component behind the toolbar and the citation tabs.

  Emitted inline and nonce'd. It runs during parse, and Alpine loads deferred, so
  the component is always defined before `x-data` is evaluated.

  d: {txt_url, url, tab_ids}
#}
{% macro script(d, nonce) %}
<script nonce="{{ nonce }}">
window.agArticleDoc = function () {
  return {
    tabs: {{ d.tab_ids|json_encode|raw }},
    tab: {{ (d.tab_ids|first)|json_encode|raw }},
    said: '',
    menu: false,
    done: '',

    /* One clipboard path for every button on the page.
       navigator.clipboard needs a secure context and is missing on some older
       Android WebViews, so the textarea fallback is not belt-and-braces — it is
       the only path on a real slice of this platform's traffic. */
    put: function (text, key, msg) {
      var self = this;
      var ok = function () {
        self.done = key;
        self.said = msg;
        window.setTimeout(function () { self.done = ''; }, 2200);
      };
      var fail = function () { self.said = 'Could not copy — select the text and copy it manually.'; };
      if (navigator.clipboard && window.isSecureContext) {
        navigator.clipboard.writeText(text).then(ok, fail);
        return;
      }
      try {
        var t = document.createElement('textarea');
        t.value = text;
        t.setAttribute('readonly', '');
        t.style.position = 'fixed';
        t.style.top = '-1000px';
        document.body.appendChild(t);
        t.select();
        var worked = document.execCommand('copy');
        document.body.removeChild(t);
        worked ? ok() : fail();
      } catch (e) { fail(); }
    },

    /* The whole document.

       Fetched from the .txt edition rather than scraped out of the DOM or inlined
       into it. Scraping would put the furniture — contents, button labels, tab
       names — on the clipboard alongside the prose; inlining would ship the whole
       document twice to every visitor for a button most never press. The fetch
       also guarantees the paste and the download are byte-identical, because they
       are the same response. */
    copyAll: function () {
      var self = this;
      this.said = 'Preparing…';
      fetch({{ d.txt_url|json_encode|raw }}, { headers: { 'Accept': 'text/plain' } })
        .then(function (r) {
          if (!r.ok) throw new Error('http ' + r.status);
          return r.text();
        })
        .then(function (t) { self.put(t, 'all', 'Full document copied to the clipboard.'); })
        .catch(function () { self.said = 'Could not fetch the text — use Download instead.'; });
    },

    copyCite: function (el) { this.put(el.innerText, 'cite', 'Citation copied.'); },
    copyLink: function () { this.put({{ d.url|json_encode|raw }}, 'link', 'Permalink copied.'); },

    /* Arrow-key movement across the citation tabs.

       Not a nicety. The tabs carry a roving tabindex — the selected one is 0, the
       rest are -1 — which is correct for a tablist and means Tab enters the group
       once instead of stopping four times. But it also means the arrow keys are
       the ONLY way to reach the others, so without this a keyboard user could see
       four citation formats and reach exactly one. */
    moveTab: function (dir) {
      var i = this.tabs.indexOf(this.tab);
      if (i < 0) i = 0;
      var next = dir === 'home' ? 0
               : dir === 'end'  ? this.tabs.length - 1
               : (i + (dir === 'next' ? 1 : -1) + this.tabs.length) % this.tabs.length;
      this.tab = this.tabs[next];
      /* Selection follows focus, so focus has to follow selection — otherwise the
         next arrow press is computed from a tab the browser is no longer on. */
      var el = document.getElementById('ar-tab-' + this.tab);
      if (el) el.focus();
    }
  };
};
</script>
{% endmacro %}
